<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-4-3-privacy-security">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Privacy and Security</title>

  <idx>privacy-security</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="data-collection-and-privacy-risks, how-systems-are-compromised,
      protecting-digital-systems, LOs-4-3, vocab-4-3"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      In this topic, we examine how personal data is collected, how systems can be compromised,
      and how digital systems and information can be protected.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="data-collection-and-privacy-risks">
    <title>Data Collection, Tracking, and Privacy Risks</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Websites, apps, and devices can collect data about user activity, including searches,
        clicks, page views, purchases, and device information. Some systems also collect or infer
        location and other details based on signals from the device or network.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Collected activity data can be used to personalize experiences, such as search results
        or recommendations, and may also be used for advertising or analytics, depending on user
        settings, company practices, and applicable laws.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Cookies are small pieces of data that a website stores in a user's browser or device to
        remember information such as login status, preferences, or items in a shopping cart.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Cookies and other identifiers can be used to recognize a user across visits and, in some
        cases, across different sites or apps, enabling measurement, personalization, and targeted
        advertising.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Geolocation data estimates or identifies a device's physical location. It can enable useful
        services, like navigation or nearby results, but can also reveal sensitive patterns, such as
        where someone lives, goes to school, works, or spends time.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.6</title>
      <p>
        Data from different sources, such as geolocation, browsing history, app activity, purchases,
        and social media, can be aggregated to create a detailed profile of an individual. Even if a
        single data point seems harmless, combining many points can reveal identity, habits, and
        relationships.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Some data is personally identifiable information (PII). Some PII is directly identifying,
        like a name or phone number, and some becomes identifying when combined with other data.
        Examples include: full name, phone number, email address, Social Security number,
        government ID numbers, precise location, and biometrics.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.A.8</title>
      <p>
        Privacy risks increase when data is stored, shared, or reused beyond the user's expectations.
        Once information is posted or shared digitally, it can be copied, forwarded, or saved by
        others, making it difficult to fully control or remove.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="how-systems-are-compromised">
    <title>How Systems and Information Can Be Compromised</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.1</title>
      <p>
        A data breach occurs when sensitive data is accessed, disclosed, or stolen by an unauthorized
        party, often because data was stored insecurely, shared improperly, or protected by weak
        security controls.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.2</title>
      <p>
        When PII is exposed in a data breach, individuals may be at risk of harms such as identity
        theft, account takeover, or fraud, especially if the data can be used to impersonate them
        or access their accounts.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.3</title>
      <p>
        Reusing the same password across multiple accounts increases the risk of account compromise
        because, if one account's password is exposed, an adversary can try the same username and
        password on other services to gain unauthorized access.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Social engineering is a strategy adversaries use to compromise systems by targeting people
        rather than exploiting a technical weakness. An adversary influences a user into taking an
        action that compromises security, often by impersonating a trusted source or creating urgency,
        fear, or curiosity.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.5</title>
      <p>
        Phishing is a type of social engineering in which an adversary attempts to influence a user
        into revealing personal information, such as passwords or verification codes. Adversaries can
        use that information to access accounts, financial services, or other protected systems.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.6</title>
      <p>
        Unsolicited emails, attachments, links, and forms can be used to gain unauthorized access by
        influencing a user into opening a file, entering information, or installing harmful software.
        These messages may come from unknown senders or from accounts that appear familiar because they
        were compromised.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.7</title>
      <p>
        Malicious links can be disguised to look trustworthy, such as by using misleading text,
        shortened URLs, or look-alike website addresses, leading users to fake pages or unsafe downloads.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.8</title>
      <p>
        Email is not inherently private. Email messages can be forwarded, stored, intercepted, or
        accessed by people other than the sender and receiver, which can expose sensitive information
        if an account or message is accessed by an unauthorized party.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.9</title>
      <p>
        Malware is software designed to harm a device or enable unauthorized access, such as by
        stealing data, monitoring user activity, or controlling parts of the system. Malware can be
        installed through unsafe downloads, malicious attachments, or compromised websites.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.B.10</title>
      <p>
        Adversaries often focus on moments when users are rushed or confused such as with urgent
        "account locked" messages. These situations increase the chance a user will click a link,
        share information, or approve access without noticing warning signs.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="protecting-digital-systems">
    <title>Protecting Digital Systems and Information</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.1</title>
      <p>
        Authentication is how a system verifies a user's identity, such as with passwords, passkeys,
        or biometrics. Strong authentication can reduce unauthorized access to devices and accounts.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.2</title>
      <p>
        Multifactor authentication (MFA) requires two or more different pieces of evidence, typically
        in at least two of the following categories: knowledge (something a user knows), possession
        (something a user has), and biometrics (something a user is). MFA adds protection because an
        attacker must compromise multiple factors, not just a single password, to access an account.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.3</title>
      <p>
        Encryption encodes data so it cannot be read without a key. Decryption decodes it so it can
        be read. Encryption helps protect data stored on devices and sent over networks.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.4</title>
      <p>
        End-to-end encryption is a method of secure communication in which data are encrypted on the
        sender's device and can only be decrypted on the intended recipient's device, so services that
        transmit or store the data along the way cannot read the contents without the decryption key.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.5</title>
      <p>
        Systems can be compromised when adversaries exploit software flaws. Installing software updates
        helps reduce risk by fixing known vulnerabilities that adversaries may target.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.6</title>
      <p>
        Malware scanning tools can help detect or block some malicious software, reducing the chance
        that a device is infected or controlled without the user's knowledge.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.7</title>
      <p>
        Apps and websites sometimes request permissions like access to location, contacts,
        microphone, camera, or photos. Reviewing and limiting permissions helps protect privacy
        and reduces the chance of misuse if an app is untrustworthy or becomes compromised.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.3.C.8</title>
      <p>
        Security measures reduce risk in different ways: authentication and MFA limit who can access
        an account, encryption limits who can read data if it is intercepted or stolen, updates reduce
        known weaknesses adversaries exploit, and permissions limit what an app can access. No single
        measure prevents all compromise, so systems often use multiple protections together.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-4-3">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>4.3.A</title>
      <p>
        Explain how personal data is collected, tracked, and aggregated in digital systems, and how these practices can create privacy risks.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.3.B</title>
      <p>
        Explain how digital systems and information can be compromised.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.3.C</title>
      <p>
        Explain how digital systems and information can be protected.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="vocab-4-3">
   <title>Vocabulary Review</title>
  </subsection>
</section>
